he big advantages are greater control  over uh nutrient placement and timing  and as a result improved efficiency  so less fertilizer may be required  uh but there are some caveats to that  and then disadvantages of course are  there there's capital investments in  injector and tanks and backflow valves  and such  and then there's some maintenance and  management  costs involved in this as well  and many of these typical fertilizers  very soluble  so  ammonium nitrate ammonium sulfate  calcium nitrate  very high solubility  potassium  materials are somewhat lower in  solubility  these solubility levels are pounds of  product per per gallon  so they're extremely high  16 pounds per  per gallon is uh  extremely high solubility i wanted to  mention that uh these levels are all  measured at the  70 degree water and solubility of all  the materials is lower the colder the  water gets so if you're using well water  you may find that this is uh  water will take maybe half as much  of these materials as you can get into  solution at 70 degrees  as uh  one precaution uh is  don't mix  calcium with sulfates or phosphates  so as if you're mixing up batch  solutions for fertigation  this is a  general rule of thumb that  these will  cause precipitation  uh and plugging problems as a result  and you're all probably familiar with a  jar test so you can always take  proportions of these materials and mix  them up in a jar to  test for  mixtures and concentrations that result  in precipitation  the fertigation  for  blueberries and brambles some general  thoughts first i think if you're new to  this you can probably reduce your annual  rate by about a third  so that's assuming there's improved  efficiency  and what i would start with is  to split that annual rate  into multiple applications  uh two to three weeks uh beginning uh  two to three weeks after bud break and  continuing through  july for blueberries and into august for  for brambles so that's that's kind of  a general  you can fertigate a  weekly or every two weeks  or you can  fertigate every time you irrigate  generally i think the efficiency that  you gain  you would gain  that would be gained from a split  application with three or four  application dates  you probably wouldn't  improve on that too much by even  continual fertigation  so  fertigation can be very efficient uh  assuming that first your irrigation  system has high uniformity  so in the midwest and the northeast  we're usually irrigating to supplement  uh  precipitation  so if your irrigation system's not  particularly uniform it's not  uh such a big deal  but if you're supplying all your  nutrition through that irrigation in the  uniform uniformity is poor then that  becomes a bigger deal  nutrients are applied when uh demand is  high so if you're uh fertigating and  you're applying  uh nutrients before bud break before the  bushes are able to  utilize nutrients then  you probably won't be achieving the  efficiency that you you could be  if you're applying them when the demand  is highest  and then uh  uh also assuming that plants are not  over irrigated  so  if you're uh  irrigating uh and pulsing uh fertilizer  through the system  and then you're  irrigating heavily afterwards it's very  easy to push that nitrogen down through  the profile and out of reach of the the  plants this was just a ditch dug across  a raspberry row  under a high tunnel so this is the  trickle irrigation system  and just  illustrate if if you  deliver your nitrogen where you should  deliver it  and then you over furl over irrigate  after that it's very easy to push it  down below  the rooting system of the the crop so  here again you'd lose the efficiency  that you might have gained  and a question about any economic  analyses of uh fertigation that's a  great question i don't know of any uh  per se  i  yeah i can't really think of any  uh something that would be useful if it  were available  so we're just about out of time and i  wanted to thank uh  marvin and kathy in particular for uh  organizing this webinar series i think  it's been real real good  so are there any um closing questions or  particularly comments  anything you'd like to discuss further  a question uh what guidelines for  irrigation uh frequency  an inch per week in summer  for example  i think each um  i would go to um  the strawberry blueberry and raspberry  uh production guides out of cornell i  think they  those publications do a great job of  summarizing that information in  detail you know in  blueberries and raspberries in michigan  we always talk about an inch of water  per week through the uh  through the season as a  rule of thumb but i think you can  generally do quite a bit better  than that if you have a little bit more  information available  question generally when blueberries are  mulch they say  to increase nitrogen rates a little  but doesn't that and get released  eventually as the mulch breaks down  or does it disappear that's another  great uh question you know in a  perennial cropping system like that  over time you would uh eventually get to  uh  equilibrium equilibrium where your  the nitrogen supplied the prior year  will be partly released and  through microbial  decomposition of the  older materials so  you're right that if you're applying a  fresh comp uh mulch over underneath the  blueberries  that effect on nitrogen availability  really is during the first year  after that you may find that you can go  back to a  rate that is consistent with a site that  didn't have mulch applied to it  now in reality a lot of growers would be  applying mulch every second third or  fourth  year so you'd have to make those  adjustments over time  bob is asking have you seen times where  applications of nitrogen  are  accidentally doubled or tripled  i  i'd probably have seen those but i  haven't known them at the time  with blueberries in particular what's  striking to me is how  slow they are to react to  fertility  so when we've done  fertilizer trials where some of the  treatments get a high rate of nitrogen  some of them get no  nitrogen  it often takes two or three or even four  years before you see a response in the  bushes  so  with these perennial plants like  blueberries and probably to some extent  raspberries  they tend to respond slowly  to good things and bad things  um  yeah charlie was asking cranberries  uh  also  uh and i assume you're asking about uh  um response to nitrogen  uh cranberries are quite a bit more  responsive to nitrogen than blueberries  so what you do in a particular year  you'll see the response to that year  yeah and a question uh um  would a cover crop  be helpful in  tying up some of the excess nitrogen  late in the season and that that's a  great uh point and it's absolutely true  so in michigan a lot of  a standard cover cropping system is  august or september seeded annual rye  and i think part of the benefit of that  rye is it tends to  soak up some of the excessive  fertility in the soil and perhaps make  that available the next spring so good  good point  i think  kathy's saying thank you so we're gonna  sign off here is that what that means  kathy  thanks so much for your attention  and uh thank you eric for  joining us today uh we'll be  we have another webinar next week  uh and kathy i'll be uh sending out uh  information on that  uh  during the week  so have a good day everyone  thanks again eric and dan talk to you  all next week


